<chapter xml:id="chapter-exp" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Exponential functions</title>

<section xml:id="section-exp-functions">
<title>Basics</title>

<example xml:id="example-exp-doubling-rate">
	<title>A doubling rate of growth</title>

	<p>
	An environmental scientist is studying the rate of population growth of a species of tree in a geographic area
	by making yearly observations of new seedlings and newly fallen trees.
	She observes that the rate of growth is increasingly dramatic,
	approximately doubling every year.
	</p>

	<figure xml:id="figure-exp-doubling-points">
		<caption>
			Data points for rate of growth of a population.
			Note that axes are not the same scale.
		</caption>
		<image width="58.5%">
			<shortdescription> Data points for rate of growth of a population. </shortdescription>
			<!-- TODO description -->
			<latex-image label="image-exp-trees-points">
				<xi:include href="exp.d/trees-points.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
		</image>
	</figure>

	<p>
	To create a mathematical model from the data points,
	the researcher fits a curve to them.
	</p>

	<figure xml:id="figure-exp-doubling-curve">
		<caption> Fitted curve for rate of growth of a population. </caption>
		<image width="58.5%">
			<shortdescription> Fitted curve for rate of growth of a population. </shortdescription>
			<!-- TODO description -->
			<latex-image label="image-exp-trees-curve">
				<xi:include href="exp.d/trees-curve.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
		</image>
	</figure>

</example>

<p>
If we assign a scale on both axes in <xref ref="figure-exp-doubling-curve" /> so that the grid lines fall at integer values,
the the plotted points fall at
<md>
	<mrow>
		r(0) \amp = 1 \amp
		r(1) \amp = 2 \amp
		r(2) \amp = 4 \amp
		r(3) \amp = 8 \amp
		r(4) \amp = 16
	</mrow>
</md>,
as the height of each data point is double the height of the preceding point,
in line with the <q>doubling every year</q> observation of the researcher,
and we can model these data points with the formula
<md> r(t) = 2^t </md>.
If we plot some additional points,
we find the fitted curve matches other values of this rate function.
</p>

<figure xml:id="figure-exp-base2-more-points">
	<caption>
		Plotting function values <m> r(-1) = 2^{-1} </m> and <m> r(1/2) = 2^{1/2} </m>
		on our <q>doubling</q> model curve.
	</caption>
	<image width="85%">
		<shortdescription> Plotting additional rate function values against our "doubling" model curve. </shortdescription>
		<!-- TODO description -->
		<latex-image label="image-exp-base2-recip-root">
			<xi:include href="exp.d/base2-recip-root.tex" parse="text" />
		</latex-image>
	</image>
</figure>

<p>
It appears that the model curve matches the values of the function <m> r(t) = 2^t </m> not only at whole, positive exponents but also at
<ul>
	<li>
		negative exponents, which are used to mean <term>reciprocal</term>:
		<md> 2^{-a} = \frac{1}{2^a} </md>
	</li>
	<li>
		rational exponents, which are used to combine a power with a root:
		<md> 2^{a/b} = \sqrt[b]{2^a} </md>.
	</li>
</ul>
We are not yet ready to attach a meaning to <em>irrational</em> exponents,
such as <md> 2^{\pi} </md>,
but for the moment we will assume that our formula <m> r(t) = 2^t </m> matches our model curve at <em>all</em> values of <m> t </m>.
</p>

<definition xml:id="definition-exp-func">
	<title>Exponential function with base <m> a </m></title>
	<statement><p>
		For positive constant <m> a </m>,
		the <term>exponential function with base <m> a </m></term> is the function <md> f(t) = c a^t </md>,
		where the constant <m> c </m> represents the initial value <m> c = f(0) </m>.
	</p></statement>
</definition>

<p>
As the constant <m> c </m> merely represents a vertical scaling and/or reflection
(if <m> c </m> is negative),
we will focus our further investigation of exponential functions on the case <m> c = 1 </m>.
</p>

<p>
Exponential functions come in three varieties:
<ul>
	<li> exponential growth when <m> a \gt 1 </m> </li>
	<li> constant when <m> a = 1 </m> </li>
	<li> exponential decay when <m> 0 \lt a \lt 1 </m>. </li>
</ul>
</p>

<figure xml:id="figure-exp-graphs">
	<caption>Graphs of exponential functions.</caption>

	<sbsgroup>

		<sidebyside widths="67.6%"><figure>
			<caption> A typical exponential growth graph. </caption>
			<image>
				<shortdescription> A typical exponential growth graph. </shortdescription>
				<!-- TODO description -->
				<latex-image label="image-exp-generic-growth-graph">
					<xi:include href="exp.d/generic-growth-graph.tex" parse="text" />
				</latex-image>
			</image>
		</figure></sidebyside>

		<sidebyside widths="50.8%"><figure>
			<caption> A typical exponential decay graph. </caption>
			<image>
				<shortdescription> A typical exponential decay graph. </shortdescription>
				<!-- TODO description -->
				<latex-image label="image-exp-generic-decay-graph">
					<xi:include href="exp.d/generic-decay-graph.tex" parse="text" />
				</latex-image>
			</image>
		</figure></sidebyside>

	</sbsgroup>

</figure>

</section>


<section xml:id="section-exp-basic-props">
<title>Basic properties of exponential functions</title>

<p> From the graphs in <xref ref="figure-exp-graphs" /> we have some obvious properties. </p>

<identity xml:id="pattern-exp-domain-range">
	<title>Domain and range</title>
	<p>
	The domain of an exponential function covers the whole real number line,
	with no singular points,
	and the range consists of all positive real numbers.
	</p>
</identity>

<identity xml:id="pattern-exp-limits">
	<title>Long-term behaviour</title>
	<p><ul>
		<li> <title> Growth </title> <p>
			When <m> a \gt 1 </m>, we have
			<md> a^t \to \infty \quad \text{ as } \quad t \to \infty </md>
			and
			<md> a^t \to 0 \quad \text{ as } \quad t \to -\infty </md>.
		</p> </li>
		<li> <title> Decay </title> <p>
			When <m> 0 \lt a \lt 1 </m>, we have
			<md> a^t \to 0 \quad \text{ as } \quad t \to \infty </md>
			and
			<md> a^t \to \infty \quad \text{ as } \quad t \to -\infty </md>.
		</p> </li>
	</ul></p>
</identity>

<p> The graphs of all exponential functions look essentially the same because they are all horizontal transformations of each other. </p>

<identity xml:id="pattern-exp-horiz-scale">
	<title>Horizontal transformations of exponential graphs</title>

	<statement><p>
		Given two positive bases <m> a </m> and <m> b </m>,
		the exponential function <m> b^t </m> is a horizontal transformation of the exponential function <m> a^t </m>:
		<md> b^t = a^{c t}\text{,} \qquad c = \frac{\ln(b)}{\ln(a)} </md>.
		Note that if <m> c </m> is negative,
		this transformation involves not only a horizontal scale but also a reflection.
	</p></statement>

	<proof>

		<p>
		Since the <q>new</q> base <m> b </m> is a positive number,
		it is in the range of the exponential function <m> a^t </m>.
		</p>

		<figure xml:id="figure-exp-new-base-on-generic-graph">
			<caption> New base as an output of an exponential function. </caption>
			<image width="67.6%">
				<shortdescription> New base as an output of an exponential function. </shortdescription>
				<!-- TODO description -->
				<latex-image label="image-exp-new-base-on-generic-graph">
					<xi:include href="exp.d/new-base-on-generic-graph.tex" parse="text" />
				</latex-image>
			</image>
		</figure>

		<p>
		We can use <xref ref="pattern-log-power" /> to determine the value of <m> t </m> so that <m> a^t = b </m>:
		<md alignment="gather">
			<mrow>       a^t = b                       </mrow>
			<mrow> \ln (a^t) = \ln (b)                 </mrow>
			<mrow>   t \ln (a) = \ln (b)               </mrow>
			<mrow>         t = \frac{\ln (b)}{\ln (a)} </mrow>
		</md>.
		Substituting <m> b = a^{\bbrac{\ln (b) / \ln (a)}} </m> into the exponential function formula <m> b^t </m>,
		we obtain
		<md> b^t = {(a^{\bbrac{\ln (b) / \ln (a)}})}^t = a^{t \bbrac{\ln (b) / \ln (a)}} </md>.
		</p>

		<figure xml:id="figure-exp-new-base-horiz-scale-graph">
			<caption> Graph of one exponential function as a horizontal scaling of another. </caption>
			<image width="75.8%">
				<shortdescription> Graph of one exponential function as a horizontal scaling of another. </shortdescription>
				<!-- TODO description -->
				<latex-image label="image-exp-new-base-horiz-scale-graph">
					<xi:include href="exp.d/new-base-horiz-scale-graph.tex" parse="text" />
				</latex-image>
			</image>
		</figure>

	</proof>

</identity>

<remark><p>
	The transformation in <xref ref="pattern-exp-horiz-scale" /> will involve a horizontal reflection precisely when one of the bases is greater than one and the other is less than one.
	That is, when one exponential function is a growth function and the other is a decay function.
</p></remark>

</section>

<section xml:id="section-exp-natural">
<title>The natural exponential function</title>

<subsection>
<title>Euler's number</title>

<p>
In <xref ref="pattern-exp-horiz-scale" />,
if we choose <m> a </m> so that <m> \ln (a) = 1 </m>,
then the comparison between the two exponential formulas becomes much simpler:
<md xml:id="equation-exp-pre-natural-comparison" tag="star"> b^t = a^{t \ln (b)} </md>.
Just like we use <m> \pi </m> to represent the special number that corresponds to the area of a unit circle,
the special number so that <m> \ln (a) = 1 </m> is given its own symbol.
</p>

<definition xml:id="definition-exp-euler">
	<title>Euler's number</title>

	<statement>

		<p>
		The real number,
		denoted by the letter <m> e </m>,
		so that <m> \ln (e) = 1 </m>.
		</p>

		<biographical valign="top">
			<title>Leonhard Euler</title>
			<p> 1707 <ndash /> 1783. </p>
		</biographical>

	</statement>

</definition>

<figure xml:id="figure-exp-euler">
	<caption> Euler's number defined relative to the special rate function <m> r(t) = 1/t </m>. </caption>
	<image width="34.7%">
		<shortdescription> Euler's number defined relative to a special rate function. </shortdescription>
		<!-- TODO description -->
		<latex-image label="image-exp-euler">
			<xi:include href="exp.d/euler.tex" parse="text" />
		</latex-image>
	</image>
</figure>

<p>
Sage knows about Euler's number <c>e</c>.
We can express it as a (truncated) decimal number using the <c>n()</c> method.
</p>

<sage>
	<input>e.n()</input>
	<output>2.71828182845905</output>
</sage>

<p>
As <m> a = e </m> is the special value that makes <xref ref="equation-exp-pre-natural-comparison" /> work,
it becomes <q>natural</q> to compare every exponential function to the exponential function with base <m> e </m>.
</p>

<definition xml:id="definition-exp-natural">
	<title>Natural exponential function</title>
	<statement><p> The function <m> \exp(t) = e^t </m>, where the constant base <m> e </m> is Euler's number. </p></statement>
</definition>

<identity xml:id="pattern-exp-scale-to-natural">
	<title>Realizing an exponential function as a horizontal transformation of the natural exponential function</title>
	<p>
	For every positive base <m> a </m>,
	we have <md> a^t = e^{t \ln (a)} </md>.
	</p>
</identity>

</subsection>

<subsection>
<title>The natural exponential function as a reflection of the natural logarithm</title>

<p>
It is possible to define the natural exponential function without using exponential notation
<mdash /> this will help us to make sense of how to compute an exponential function's output for irrational inputs.
Consider what happens when we reflect the graph of the natural logarithm in the line <m> y = t </m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="figure-exp-reflect-ln">
	<caption> A reflection of the graph of the natural logarithm function. </caption>
	<image width="88.8%">
		<shortdescription> A reflection of the graph of the natural logarithm function. </shortdescription>
		<!-- TODO description -->
		<latex-image label="image-exp-reflect-ln">
			<xi:include href="exp.d/reflect-ln.tex" parse="text" />
		</latex-image>
	</image>
</figure>

<p>
This reflected curve passes the <xref ref="pattern-funcs-VLT" text="title" />,
and so it is the graph of some function.
Indeed, it looks like the graph of an exponential function,
but which one?
Let's explore by plotting some points.
A graph <m> y = f(t) </m> is a collection of input-output pairs <m> (t,y) </m>.
Reflecting in the line <m> y = t </m> interchanges the coordinates in a point,
so that if <m> (t,y) </m> is on the graph <m> y = \ln (t) </m>,
then <m> (y,t) </m> is on the reflected curve.
For example,
from <xref ref="pattern-log-value-at-1" />,
we have
<md alignment="gather">
	<mrow> \ln (1) = 0 </mrow>
	<mrow> \implies (1,0) \text{ on graph } y = \ln (t) </mrow>
	<mrow> \implies (0,1) \text{ on reflected curve } </mrow>
</md>.
Now, the point <m> (0,1) </m> is on the graph of <em>every</em> exponential function,
as <m> a^0 = 1 </m> for each positive base <m> a </m>.
So let's consider some other points.
Using <xref ref="definition-exp-euler" /> along with <xref ref="pattern-log-power" />,
we have
<md> \ln (e^r) = r \ln (e) = r \cdot 1 </md>,
so
<md alignment="gather">
	<mrow> \ln (e^r) = r </mrow>
	<mrow> \implies (e^r,r) \text{ on graph } y = \ln (t) </mrow>
	<mrow> \implies (r,e^r) \text{ on reflected curve } </mrow>
</md>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="figure-exp-points-on-reflect-ln">
	<caption> Specific points on a reflection of the graph of the natural logarithm function. </caption>
	<image width="88.8%">
		<shortdescription> Specific points on a reflection of the graph of the natural logarithm function. </shortdescription>
		<!-- TODO description -->
		<latex-image label="image-exp-points-reflect-ln">
			<xi:include href="exp.d/points-reflect-ln.tex" parse="text" />
		</latex-image>
	</image>
</figure>

<p>
Since <m> (r,e^r) </m> is on the reflected curve for all values of <m> r </m>,
we can say that this is indeed the graph of the natural exponential function.
</p>

<identity xml:id="pattern-exp-as-reflection">
	<title>The natural exponential function as a reflection of the natural logarithm function</title>
	<p>
	The natural exponential function <m> \exp(t) = e^t </m> is the unique function whose graph is the reflection in the line <m> y = t </m> of the graph of the natural logarithm function <m> \ln (t) </m>.
	</p>
</identity>

<remark><p>
	We will further explore pairs of functions whose graphs are reflections of each other in the line <m> y = t </m>,
	similarly to the natural exponential and logarithm functions,
	in <xref ref="chapter-comp-inv" />.
</p></remark>

</subsection>

<subsection>
<title>Computing values of exponential functions</title>

<p>
Realizing the natural exponential function as a reflection of the natural logarithm function,
as in <xref ref="pattern-exp-as-reflection" />,
tells us (in principle) how to compute expressions like <m> e^\pi </m>.
</p>

<identity xml:id="pattern-exp-computing-via-ln">
	<title>Using the natural logarithm to compute values of the natural exponential function</title>
	<p>
	The following two equalities are equivalent to each other.
	<md><mrow> e^w \amp = z \amp \ln (z) \amp = w </mrow></md>.
	</p>
</identity>

<p>
<xref ref="pattern-exp-computing-via-ln" /> says that computing <m> e^\pi </m> is equivalent to solving <m> \ln (z) = \pi </m> for <m> z </m>.
In the Sage cell below, change the value of the <c>z</c> variable
(which is used as the upper bound of the integration command)
and re-evaluate until you get reasonably close to the value of <m> \pi </m>.
(Recall that <m> \ln (t) </m> is defined as an integral of rate function <m> r(t) = 1/t </m>.)
The first command in the Sage cell below prints out a numerical approximation of <m> \pi </m>.
</p>

<sage>
	<input>
		var('t')
		print("pi =", pi.n())
		z = 1
		result = integrate(1/t,t,1,z)
		print("ln", z, "=", result.n())
	</input>
	<output>
		pi = 3.14159265358979
		ln 1 = 0.000000000000000
	</output>
</sage>

<p> Of course, computers and calculators know how to (approximately) compute values of <m> e^t </m> for arbitrary values of <m> t </m>. </p>

<sage>
	<input>
		result = e^pi
		print(result.n())
	</input>
	<output> 23.1406926327793 </output>
</sage>

<p>
We can combine <xref ref="pattern-exp-computing-via-ln" /> with <xref ref="pattern-exp-scale-to-natural" /> and use a similar method to compute values like
<md> 2^\pi = e^{\pi \ln (2)} </md>
by solving
<md> \ln (z) = \pi \ln (2) </md>.
Use the Sage cell below to do just that.
As before,
you are attempting to refine the value of the <c>z</c> variable until the integration result is very close to <m> \pi \ln (2) </m>,
which the first command computes for you.
</p>

<sage>
	<input>
		var('t')
		print("pi * ln(2) =", (pi * ln(2)).n())
		z = 1
		result = integrate(1/t,t,1,z)
		print("ln", z, "=", result.n())
	</input>
	<output>
		pi * ln(2) = 2.17758609030360
		ln 1 = 0.000000000000000
	</output>
</sage>

<p>
When you've had enough of trial-and-error attempts to determine the value of <m> 2^\pi </m> above,
you can ask Sage to compute it for you below.
</p>

<sage>
	<input>
		result = 2^pi
		print(result.n())
	</input>
	<output> 8.82497782707629 </output>
</sage>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-exp-inverse-ln">
<title>Undoing the natural exponential and logarithm functions</title>

<p>
The natural exponential and logarithm functions are not only graphical reflections of each other,
they <q>reverse</q> each other algebraically,
just as <em>cube</em> and <em>cube root</em> reverse each other.
</p>

<aside valign="middle">
	<title>Warning</title>
	<p>
	<em>Square</em> and <em>square root</em> do <alert>not</alert> <em>perfectly</em> reverse each other in the same way that exponential/logarithm or cube/cube root do.
	For example, in a perfect reversal we would expect <m> \sqrt{(-1)^2} </m> to evaluate to the starting value of <m> -1 </m>,
	but in fact <m> \sqrt{(-1)^2} = 1 </m>.
	We will account for this mismatch in <xref ref="chapter-comp-inv" />.
	</p>
</aside>

<identity xml:id="pattern-exp-ln-inverse">
	<title>Composing natural exponential and logarithm</title>
	<p><ol>

		<li xml:id="pattern-exp-ln-inverse-exp-inside-ln"><title>Exponential inside logarithm</title><p>
			For all <m> a </m>,
			we have <md> \ln (e^a) = a </md>.
		</p></li>

		<li xml:id="pattern-exp-ln-inverse-ln-inside-exp"><title>Logarithm inside exponential</title><p>
			For all <em>positive</em> <m> a </m>,
			we have <md> e^{\ln (a)} = a </md>.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p>
</identity>

<p>
We can use the patterns above to solve equations involving <m> \exp </m> and <m> \ln </m>.
Before we do examples, we continue the analogy with solving equations involving cube and cube root.
Suppose you wanted to solve the equation <md> \sqrt[3]{t + 1} = 2 </md>.
The first step is to <q>undo</q> the cube root operation by cubing,
to free the variable <m> t </m> from being trapped inside the cube root operation.
But to maintain the equality, we must cube both sides:
<md>
	<mrow> {(\sqrt[3]{t + 1})}^3 = 2^3 </mrow>
	<mrow> t + 1 = 8 </mrow>
	<mrow> t = 7 </mrow>
</md>.
Similarly, to solve the equation <md> {(t + 1)}^3 = 2 </md>,
the first step is to <q>undo</q> the cube operation on the left by taking the cube root of both sides:
<md>
	<mrow> \sqrt[3]{{(t + 1})^3} = \sqrt[3]{2} </mrow>
	<mrow> t + 1 = \sqrt[3]{2} </mrow>
	<mrow> t = \sqrt[3]{2} - 1 </mrow>
</md>.
We can perform similar algebra using <m> \exp </m> and <m> \ln </m>.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-exp-solving-exp">
	<title>Solving an equation involving the natural exponential function</title>
	<p>
	To solve the equation <md> e^{t + 1} = 2 </md>,
	we first <q>undo</q> the exponential by taking the logarithm of both sides and applying
	<xref ref="pattern-exp-ln-inverse-exp-inside-ln" text="type-local">Property</xref>
	of <xref ref="pattern-exp-ln-inverse" />:
	<md>
		<mrow> \ln (e^{t + 1}) = \ln (2) </mrow>
		<mrow> t + 1 = \ln (2) </mrow>
		<mrow> t = \ln (2) - 1 </mrow>
	</md>.
	</p>
</example>

<p>
Since every exponential is really just a horizontal transformation of the natural one,
we can use the same technique to solve an equation involving any exponential.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-exp-solving-different-base-exp">
	<title>Solving an equation involving an exponential function</title>
	<p>
	To solve the equation <md> a^{t + 1} = 2 </md>,
	we first take the logarithm of both sides and apply <xref ref="pattern-log-power" />:
	<md>
		<mrow> \ln (a^{t + 1}) = \ln (2) </mrow>
		<mrow> (t + 1) \ln (a) = \ln (2) </mrow>
		<mrow> t + 1 = \frac{\ln (2)}{\ln (a)} </mrow>
		<mrow> t = \frac{\ln (2)}{\ln (a)} - 1 </mrow>
	</md>.
	</p>
</example>

<p> Finally, an example with the roles of <m> \exp </m> and <m> \ln </m> reversed. </p>

<example xml:id="example-exp-solving-ln">
	<title>Solving an equation involving the natural logarithm function</title>
	<p>
	To solve the equation <md> \ln (t + 1) = 2 </md>,
	we first <q>undo</q> the logarithm by taking the natural exponential of both sides and applying
	<xref ref="pattern-exp-ln-inverse-ln-inside-exp" text="type-local">Property</xref>
	of <xref ref="pattern-exp-ln-inverse" />:
	<md>
		<mrow> e^{\ln (t + 1)} = e^2 </mrow>
		<mrow> t + 1 = e^2 </mrow>
		<mrow> t = e^2 - 1 </mrow>
	</md>.
	</p>
</example>

</subsection>

</section>

<section xml:id="section-exp-other-logs">
<title>Logarithms in other bases</title>

<p>
Every exponential function is a horizontal scaling
(possibly including a reflection)
of the natural exponential function.
(See <xref ref="pattern-exp-scale-to-natural" />.)
In the reflection in the line <m> y = t </m> between the graphs of <m> \exp(t) </m> and <m> \ln(t) </m>,
a horizontal scaling of <m> \exp(t) </m> corresponds to a <em>vertical</em> scaling of <m> \ln(t) </m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="figure-exp-reflect-hscale-of-exp">
	<caption> A reflection of the graph of a horizontal scaling of the natural exponential function. </caption>
	<image width="88.8%">
		<shortdescription> A reflection of the graph of a horizontal scaling of the natural exponential function. </shortdescription>
		<!-- TODO description -->
		<latex-image label="image-exp-reflect-hscale-exp">
			<xi:include href="exp.d/reflect-hscale-exp.tex" parse="text" />
		</latex-image>
	</image>
</figure>

<p>
We know that <md>a^t = \exp\bbrac{t \ln(a)}</md>,
a horizontal scaling by scale factor <m> \ln(a) </m>.
In the case that <m> a \gt e </m>
(so that <m> \ln(a) \gt 1 </m>),
the scaling will be a compression,
as pictured in <xref ref="figure-exp-reflect-hscale-of-exp" />.
This corresponds to a vertical compression of the graph of <m> \ln(t) </m>,
by the same scale factor.
But to achieve a <em>vertical</em> compression,
we must <em>divide</em> instead of multiply.
Therefore,
the scaled version of the natural logarithm pictured in <xref ref="figure-exp-reflect-hscale-of-exp" /> is
<md> \ell(t) = \frac{\ln(t)}{\ln(a)} </md>.
</p>

<definition xml:id="definition-exp-log-base-a">
	<title>Base-<m> a </m> logarithm</title>
	<statement><p>
		For <m> a \gt 0 </m>,
		define the <term>base-<m> a </m> logarithm</term> to be the function
		<md> \log_a(t) = \frac{\ln(t)}{\ln(a)} </md>.
	</p></statement>
</definition>

<note><p>
	Since <m> \ln(e) = 1 </m>, a logarithm in base <m> a = e </m> is just the natural logarithm function <m> \ln(t) </m>.
</p></note>

<p>
Every logarithm function enjoys the same properties as the natural logarithm described in
<xref ref="section-log-props" />.
And the relationship between logarithm and exponential of the same base also carries over.
</p>

<identity xml:id="pattern-exp-base-a-ln-inverse">
	<title>Composing natural exponential and logarithm</title>
	<p>
	Assume <m> a \gt 0 </m>.
	<ol>

		<li xml:id="pattern-exp-base-a-ln-inverse-exp-inside-ln"><title>Exponential inside logarithm</title><p>
			For all <m> t </m>,
			we have <md> \log_a (a^t) = t </md>.
		</p></li>

		<li xml:id="pattern-exp-base-a-ln-inverse-ln-inside-exp"><title>Logarithm inside exponential</title><p>
			For all <em>positive</em> <m> t </m>,
			we have <md> a^{\log_a(t)} = t </md>.
		</p></li>

	</ol>
	</p>
</identity>

<p> In particular, note that <m> \log_a (a) = 1 </m>. </p>

</section>

<section xml:id="section-exp-more-props">
<title>More properties of exponential functions</title>

<p>
Now that we have established the <q>reflection</q> relationship between the natural exponential and logarithm functions,
we can <q>reflect</q> the properties from <xref ref="section-log-props" /> to obtain further properties of exponential functions.
</p>

<identity xml:id="pattern-exp-sum">
	<title>Exponential of a sum</title>

	<statement><p>
		For positive base <m> a </m>,
		we have <md> a^{u + v} = a^u a^v </md>.
		In other words, <em>the exponential of a sum is the product of the exponentials</em>.
	</p></statement>

	<proof><p>
		From <xref ref="pattern-exp-scale-to-natural" />,
		we have
		<md> a^{u + v} = e^{(u + v) \bbrac{\ln (a)}} </md>.
		From <xref ref="pattern-exp-computing-via-ln" />,
		<m> z = e^{(u + v) \bbrac{\ln (a)}} </m> should be the unique value so that <m> \ln (z) = (u + v) \bbrac{\ln (a)} </m>.
		Let's check whether applying the natural logarithm to <m> a^u a^v </m> evaluates to the same result.
		Using the properties of the natural logarithm,
		we have
		<md>
			<mrow> \ln (a^u a^v) \amp = \ln (a^u) + \ln (a^v) </mrow>
			<mrow> \amp = u \ln (a) + v \ln (a) </mrow>
			<mrow> \amp = (u + v) \ln (a) </mrow>
		</md>,
		as desired.
	</p></proof>

</identity>

<remark><p>
	Compare patterns:
	<ul>
		<li><title><xref ref="pattern-exp-sum" /></title><p>
			The exponential of a sum is the product of the exponentials.
		</p></li>
		<li><title><xref ref="pattern-log-product" /></title><p>
			The logarithm of a product is the sum of the logarithms.
		</p></li>
	</ul>
	The <q>reflection</q> here is the interchanging of the words <em>sum</em> and <em>product</em>.
	Try to identify the <q>reflection</q> in each of the next two properties,
	compared to the corresponding property in <xref ref="section-log-props" />.
	Also try to provide a justification for each of these two properties in the same manner as the justification for <xref ref="pattern-exp-scale-to-natural" />.
</p></remark>

<identity xml:id="pattern-exp-negative">
	<title>Exponential of a negative</title>
	<p>
	For positive base <m> a </m>,
	we have <md> a^{- u} = \frac{1}{a^u} </md>.
	In other words, <em>the exponential of a negative is the reciprocal of the exponential</em>.
	</p>
</identity>

<identity xml:id="pattern-exp-diff">
	<title>Exponential of a difference</title>
	<p>
	For positive base <m> a </m>,
	we have <md> a^{u - v} = \frac{a^u}{a^v} </md>.
	In other words, <em>the exponential of a difference is the quotient of the exponentials</em>.
	</p>
</identity>

<p> Here is another algebraic property that doesn't have a corresponding <q>reflected</q> property in <xref ref="section-log-props" />. </p>

<identity xml:id="pattern-exp-exp">
	<title>Composing exponentials</title>

	<statement><p>
		For positive base <m> a </m>,
		we have <md> {(a^u)}^v = a^{u v} </md>.
	</p></statement>

	<proof><p>
		From <xref ref="pattern-exp-scale-to-natural" />,
		we have
		<md> <mrow> {(a^u)}^v \amp = e^{v \ln (a^u)} \amp a^{u v} \amp = e^{(u v) \ln (a)} </mrow> </md>.
		But from <xref ref="pattern-log-power" /> we have <md> \ln (a^u) = u \ln (a) </md>,
		so the two exponential expressions above are indeed the same.
	</p></proof>

</identity>

<note><p>
	<xref ref="pattern-exp-limits" /> could also be considered a <q>reflection</q> of the corresponding logarithmic pattern
	<xref ref="pattern-log-limits" />.
	When we reflect in the line <m> y = t </m>,
	we reflect the horizontal axis onto the vertical axis,
	and vice versa.
	So inputs become outputs and outputs become inputs.
	If we view the pattern
	<md> t \to 0^+ \qquad \implies \qquad \ln (t) \to -\infty </md>
	from <xref ref="pattern-log-limits" /> as
	<md> \text{input} \to 0^+ \qquad \text{then} \qquad \text{output} \to -\infty </md>,
	then the <q>reflected</q> pattern for the exponential function is
	<md> \text{output} \to 0^+ \qquad \text{when} \qquad \text{input} \to -\infty </md>,
	so that
	<md> t \to -\infty \qquad \implies \qquad \exp t \to 0^+ </md>,
	meaning that the exponential graph heads towards <m> 0 </m> from above as the input goes off to the left.
</p></note>

</section>


<section xml:id="section-exp-deriv">
<title>Derivatives of exponential functions</title>

<subsection>
<title>Derivative of the natural exponential function</title>

<p>
Let's return to our visualization of the graph of the natural exponential function as a reflection of the graph of the natural logarithm function.
Additionally,
we have drawn on some tangent lines,
one to each curve.
</p>

<figure xml:id="figure-exp-reflect-ln-with-tangents">
	<caption>
		The natural exponential function as a reflection of the graph of the natural logarithm function,
		with corresponding tangent lines on each curve.
	</caption>

	<sbsgroup>

		<sidebyside width="80.5%"><figure xml:id="figure-exp-reflect-ln-with-tangents-ln">
			<caption> A tangent line to the natural logarithm function. </caption>
			<image>
				<shortdescription>
					The graphs of the natural exponential and logarithm functions,
					with tangent lines.
				</shortdescription>
				<!-- TODO description -->
				<latex-image label="image-exp-reflect-ln-with-ln-tangent">
					<xi:include href="exp.d/reflect-ln-with-ln-tangent.tex" parse="text" />
				</latex-image>
			</image>
		</figure></sidebyside>

		<sidebyside width="53.2%"><figure xml:id="figure-exp-reflect-ln-with-tangents-exp">
			<caption> A tangent line to the natural exponential function. </caption>
			<image>
				<shortdescription>
					The graphs of the natural exponential and logarithm functions,
					with tangent lines.
				</shortdescription>
				<!-- TODO description -->
				<latex-image label="image-exp-reflect-ln-with-exp-tangent">
					<xi:include href="exp.d/reflect-ln-with-exp-tangent.tex" parse="text" />
				</latex-image>
			</image>
		</figure></sidebyside>

	</sbsgroup>

</figure>

<p>
The <em>rise-over-run</em> formula can be used between any two points on a line to compute its slope.
For the tangent line to the natural logarithm function in <xref ref="figure-exp-reflect-ln-with-tangents-ln" />,
we have arbitrarily chosen two points on the tangent line and labelled the <em>run</em> between them as <m> \change{t} </m> and the <em>rise</em> between them as <m> \change{y} </m>,
so that the line's slope is <m> \inlineslope{y}{t} </m>.
The slope of this tangent line also has a relationship to the input point <m> t^\ast </m> at which the tangent line meets the curve,
given by <xref ref="pattern-log-deriv" />.
So we can say that
<md> \slope{y}{t} = \frac{1}{t^\ast} </md>.
</p>

<p>
By symmetry, in <xref ref="figure-exp-reflect-ln-with-tangents-exp" /> the <em>output</em> point <m> y^\ast </m> should have exactly the same relationship to <m> \change{t} </m> and <m> \change{y} </m>,
under the following correspondences
through the reflection <m> t \leftrightarrow y </m>.
(In these correspondences, the left-hand side is from <xref ref="figure-exp-reflect-ln-with-tangents-ln" /> and the right-hand side is from <xref ref="figure-exp-reflect-ln-with-tangents-exp" />.)
<md><mrow>
	t^\ast \amp \leftrightarrow y^\ast
	\amp
	\change{t} \amp \leftrightarrow \change{y}
	\amp
	\change{y} \amp \leftrightarrow \change{t}
</mrow></md>
So we have
<md> \slope{t}{y} = \frac{1}{y^\ast} </md>.
But now this slope is <em>run-over-rise</em> instead of <em>rise-over-run</em>;
to get the correct slope for the tangent line in <xref ref="figure-exp-reflect-ln-with-tangents-exp" />,
we need to flip things:
<md> \slope{y}{t} = y^\ast </md>.
Since <m> y^\ast </m> is an <em>output</em> for the natural exponential function,
we obtain the following derivative formula for the natural exponential function.
</p>

<identity xml:id="pattern-exp-deriv">
	<title>Derivative of the natural exponential function</title>
	<p>
	The natural exponential function is differentiable for all <m> t </m>,
	with <md> \ddt{e^t} = e^t </md>.
	In function notation, this says that <md> \exp'(t) = \exp(t) </md>.
	</p>
</identity>

<p>
This is a remarkable fact!
It says that a quantity that experiences exponential growth will also have a <em>rate</em> of growth that grows exponentially.
This is why exponential growth can be both exciting
(when considering compounding of gains in your investment account, for example)
and terrifying
(when considering unchecked spread of a virus in a population, for example):
the more there is, the sooner there will be a lot more than that.
</p>

<example>
	<title>Derivative involving an exponential</title>
	<p>
	For <md> q(t) = t^5 + 6 e^t - 24 </md>,
	we have
	<md>

		<mrow> q'(t) \amp = \opddt \bbrac{t^5 + 6 e^t - 24} </mrow>

		<mrow> \amp = \ddt{t^5} + \ddt{(6 e^t)} - \ddt{24} </mrow>

		<mrow> \amp = 5 t^4 + 6 \ddt{e^t} + 0 </mrow>

		<mrow> \amp = 5 t^4 + 6 e^t </mrow>

	</md>
	where in the first step we have applied the
	<xref ref="pattern-deriv-sum" text="custom">Sum Rule</xref>
	and the <xref ref="pattern-deriv-diff" text="custom">Difference Rule</xref>,
	in the second step we have applied the
	<xref ref="pattern-poly-deriv-power" text="custom">Power Rule</xref>,
	the <xref ref="pattern-deriv-scale" text="custom">Constant Multiple Rule</xref>,
	and the rule for the <xref ref="pattern-deriv-constant" text="custom">derivative of a constant</xref>,
	and in the final step we have applied our new derivative formula for the natural exponential.
	</p>
</example>

</subsection>

<subsection>
<title>Exponential functions as solutions to proportional differential equations</title>

<p>
We can now combine <xref ref="pattern-exp-deriv" /> with <xref ref="pattern-deriv-h-scale" /> to verify exponential solutions to differential equations
with form similar to our <xref ref="example-models-radioactive-decay" text="title" /> example (<xref ref="example-models-radioactive-decay" />),
where the rate is proportional to the quantity.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-exp-solution-to-proportional-diff-equation">
	<title>Verifying an exponential solution to a proportional differential equation</title>

	<p>
	Suppose <m> q(t) </m> is a quantity function whose rate of accumulation is proportional to the quantity,
	with proportionality constant <m> k </m>.
	For the purposes of this example,
	let's take our proportionality constant to be <m> k = 3 </m>,
	so that
	<md xml:id="equation-exp-proportional-diff-eqn" tag="star"> \dqdt = 3 q(t) </md>.
	So we are looking for a function whose derivative is essentially the same function,
	just vertically scaled by <m> 3 </m>.
	The pattern <md> \ddt{e^t} = e^t </md> is almost correct,
	but here the vertical scale factor between function and derivative is only <m> 1 </m>.
	However,
	we know from <xref ref="pattern-deriv-h-scale" /> that introducing a horizontal scale factor into the quantity function also introduces a vertical scale factor into the derivative function.
	In this case,
	if we take <md> q(t) = \exp(3 t) </md>,
	then <xref ref="pattern-deriv-h-scale" /> says that
	<md>
		<mrow> q'(t) \amp = 3 \exp'(3 t) </mrow>
		<mrow> \amp = 3 \exp(3 t) </mrow>
		<mrow> \amp = 3 q(t) </mrow>
	</md>.
	This shows that <m> q(t) = e^{3 t} </m> is a solution to the differential equation in
	<xref ref="equation-exp-proportional-diff-eqn" />.
	</p>

	<p>
	This is only one <term>particular solution</term> to <xref ref="equation-exp-proportional-diff-eqn" />.
	Can we guess at the general solution by introducing some parameter?
	Our particular solution above has initial value <md> q(0) = e^{3 \cdot 0} = 1 </md>.
	But exponential growth or decay does not turn into some other behaviour if there is a different initial value <mdash />
	otherwise a horizontal shift (so that the point in time that is considered <m> t = 0 </m> is shifted)
	would change the type of graph,
	which is not possible.
	For example,
	see our direction field for <m> k = -1/2 </m>
	(<xref ref="figure-models-radioactive-int-curves" />)
	<mdash /> the example solution curves all look like exponential decay no matter the initial value.
	</p>

	<p>
	Recall from <xref ref="definition-exp-func" /> that introducing a constant multiple in an exponential function changes the initial amount.
	So let's try <md> q(t) = A e^{3 t} </md>,
	where <m> A </m> represents the initial amount <m> A = q(0) </m>.
	Using the <xref ref="pattern-deriv-scale" text="custom">Constant Multiple Rule</xref>
	(<xref ref="pattern-deriv-scale" />)
	for our first step,
	we have
	<md>
		<mrow> q'(t) \amp = \opddt \bbrac{A \exp(3 t)} </mrow>
		<mrow> \amp = A \opddt \bbrac{\exp(3 t)} </mrow>
		<mrow> \amp = A \bbrac{3 \exp'(3 t)} </mrow>
		<mrow> \amp = 3 A \exp(3 t) </mrow>
		<mrow> \amp = 3 q(t) </mrow>
	</md>.
	This verifies that for <em>every</em> possible initial value <m> A </m>,
	the exponential function <m> q(t) = A e^{3 t} </m>
	is a solution to the rate equation in <xref ref="equation-exp-proportional-diff-eqn" />.
	</p>

</example>

<p>
Our analysis at the end of <xref ref="example-exp-solution-to-proportional-diff-equation" />
doesn't verify that <em>every</em> solution has that form,
but it strongly suggests it:
given any initial amount <m> A = q(0) </m> we have a solution function <m> q(t) = A e^{k t} </m> that fits the behaviour of the differential equation,
and it doesn't seem possible for there to exist some other solution function <m> q(t) </m> with the same <m> q(0) </m> value,
as that would mean the system would somehow have to initially <q>decide</q> which of those two solution curves to follow.
For now we will at least record the general pattern that we've found without overstating our current knowledge,
but in <xref ref="chapter-substitution" /> we will be able to verify that we in fact have the general solution.
</p>

<identity xml:id="pattern-exp-proportional-diff-eqn-gen-soln">
	<title>Solutions to a proportional differential equation</title>
	<p>
	Consider differential equation <md> \dqdt = k q(t) </md>,
	where the rate of accumulation is proportional to the quantity by constant of proportionality <m> k </m>.
	Then every function of the form <md> q(t) = A e^{k t} </md> is a solution to the differential equation,
	where the parameter <m> A </m> represents the initial quantity <m> q(0) </m>.
	</p>
</identity>

<remark>
	<title>Calculations versus reality</title>
	<p>
	In the discussion between <xref ref="example-exp-solution-to-proportional-diff-equation" />
	and <xref ref="pattern-exp-proportional-diff-eqn-gen-soln" />,
	we asserted the plausible-sounding principle that a system should always behave exactly the same way given the exact same initial conditions.
	In reality,
	unless you are counting molecules,
	it is nearly impossible to re-run an experiment with <em>exactly</em> the same initial conditions.
	And we will find in <xref ref="chapter-auto" /> that sometimes a particular initial quantity value <m> A = q(0) </m> can be <term>unstable</term>,
	in the sense that the system behaves very differently depending on whether the actual <m> q(0) </m> value is slightly greater than <m> A </m> or slightly less then <m> A </m>.
	</p>
</remark>

<example>
	<title>Radioactive decay</title>

	<p>
	Suppose <m> q(t) </m> measures how many kilograms of a <quantity><mag>3</mag><unit base="gram" prefix="kilo"/></quantity> hunk of radioactive material remain after <m> t </m> years.
	For this particular material,
	it is experimentally determined that its rate of decay can be modelled as <md> \dqdt = - \frac{q(t)}{2} </md>.
	This is the same differential equation we analysed graphically in <xref ref="example-models-exp-decay-dir-field" />,
	and from the direction field produced there we expect solutions to have the <q>shape</q> of exponential decay.
	Based on our analysis in <xref ref="example-exp-solution-to-proportional-diff-equation" />,
	we know that quantity function <m> q(t) = 3 e^{- t / 2} </m> is a solution to this differential equation,
	where we have used the initial amount <m> q(0) = 3 </m> as the value of parameter <m> A </m>,
	and the proportionality constant <m> k = - 1 /2 </m> from the differential equation.
	</p>

	<p>
	We can use the solution function to determine properties of the material,
	such as its <term>half-life</term>,
	which is the time it takes for the sample to be reduced by half.
	Our initial quantity was <m> q(0) = 3 </m>,
	so we are looking to solve for the <m> t </m>-value that satisfies <md> q(t) = 3 / 2 = 1.5 </md>.
	As in <xref ref="subsection-exp-inverse-ln" />,
	we can use the natural logarithm to help us solve this.
	<md alignment="gather">
		<mrow> 3 e^{- t / 2} = \frac{3}{2} </mrow>
		<mrow> e^{- t / 2} = \frac{1}{2} </mrow>
		<mrow> \ln(e^{- t / 2}) = \ln\left(\frac{1}{2}\right) </mrow>
		<mrow> - \frac{t}{2} = - \ln(2) </mrow>
		<mrow> t = 2 \ln(2) </mrow>
	</md>
	So it will take approximately <m> 1.4 </m> years for the sample to be reduced by half.
	</p>

</example>

<remark>
	<title>Half-life is independent of initial amount</title>
	<p>
	For differential equation <md> \dqdt = -k q(t) </md>
	(where here we have explicitly included a negative sign and assume that the constant <m> k </m> is positive,
	so that overall the constant of proportionality is negative and we have <term>decay</term>),
	the half-life depends <em>only</em> on <m> k </m> and not on the initial amount <m> A = q(0) </m>.
	That is,
	it will take the same amount of time for a sample to be reduced by half,
	regardless of how large or small of a sample.
	This is because we are looking to solve
	<md> A e^{- k t} = \frac{A}{2} \qquad \implies e^{- k t} = \frac{1}{2} </md>,
	where the value of <m> A </m> is no longer involved after one step of simplification.
	</p>
</remark>

<exercise>
	<title>Half-life formula</title>
	<p>
	Determine a formula in <m> k </m> for the half-life of a radioactive material that decays according to
	<md> \dqdt = -k q(t) \text{,} \qquad k \gt 0 \text{.} </md>
	</p>
</exercise>

</subsection>

<subsection>
<title>Derivatives of general exponential functions</title>

<p>
Since every exponential function is a horizontal scaling of the natural exponential function
(<xref ref="pattern-exp-scale-to-natural" />),
we may combine our derivative formula for the natural exponential function
(<xref ref="pattern-exp-deriv" />)
with our rule for the derivative of a horizontally-scaled function
(<xref ref="pattern-deriv-h-scale" />).
</p>

<identity xml:id="pattern-exp-general-deriv">
	<title>Derivative of a general exponential function</title>

	<statement><p>
		Every exponential function with positive base is differentiable for all <m> t </m>,
		with <md> \ddt{a^t} = a^t \ln(a) </md>.
	</p></statement>

	<proof><p>
		Consider <m> q(t) = a^t </m> with <m> a \gt 0 </m>.
		The formula we would like to verify is
		<md> q'(t) = q(t) \cdot k \quad \text{for} \quad k = \ln(a) </md>.
		With this same value of <m> k </m>,
		<xref ref="pattern-exp-scale-to-natural" /> says that
		<md> q(t) = \exp(k t) </md>.
		Applying <xref ref="pattern-deriv-h-scale" /> with <m> \exp </m> in place of <m> p </m> gives us
		<md> q'(t) = k \exp'(k t) </md>.
		But <m> \exp'(t) = \exp(t) </m>,
		so
		<md> q'(t) = k \exp(k t) = k q(t) </md>,
		our desired result.
	</p></proof>

</identity>

</subsection>

</section>


<section xml:id="section-exp-int">
<title>Integrals of exponential functions</title>

<p>
Suppose we have an exponential rate function, <m> r(t) = e^t </m>.
This means that the <em>rate</em> of accumulation of some quantity is itself growing exponentially,
similar to the <em>rate doubles every year</em> pattern in <xref ref="example-exp-doubling-rate" />.
We would like to know the associated accumulation function based at <m> t = 0 </m>,
<md> A_0(t) = \ccmint{0}{t}{e^u}{u} </md>.
</p>

<p>
For power functions we have found that the pattern of <em>integrate-then-differentiate</em> returns us back to the original function.
Here we remind ourselves of that pattern,
in general for power functions with positive exponent <m> m \gt 0 </m>,
and also specifically for the power function with exponent <m> m = -1 </m> whose integral defines the natural logarithm function.
<md><mrow>
	\opddt \, \ccmint{0}{t}{ u^m }{t} \amp = t^m
	\amp
	\opddt \, \ccmint{0}{t}{ \frac{1}{u} }{t} \amp = \frac{1}{t}
</mrow></md>
If we expect this pattern to also hold for exponential functions,
then we should have
<md> \opddt \ccmint{0}{t}{ e^u }{t} = e^t </md>,
or
<md> \ddt{A_0(t)} = e^t </md>.
What kind of function has an exponential derivative?
Well, the natural exponential function does (<xref ref="pattern-exp-deriv" />)!
But we have to be careful:
just as recording only speedometer data will not tell you where the vehicle ended up at the end of the trip,
knowing only a derivative calculation result does not tell you the original function exactly.
You also need to take into account the initial condition.
</p>

<p>
For now,
we'll ask Sage to help us find the pattern in the accumulation values for an exponential rate function.
In the Sage cell below,
try different values for the variable upper bound <c>t</c> and look for the pattern.
</p>

<sage>
	<input>
		var('u')
		t = 1
		integrate(e^u,u,0,t)
	</input>
	<output>e^t - 1</output>
</sage>

<p>
As expected,
an exponential rate of accumulation leads to an exponential accumulation function.
The extra <q>minus one</q> is essentially a correction for the fact that an exponential function has initial value <m> e^0 = 1 </m>,
whereas accumulation should always be zero if no time has elapsed.
</p>

<p>
In the case that the exponential rate function has a different base,
we also need a <em>vertical scale</em> correction factor.
(Compare with the vertical scale factor in the derivative formula of a general exponential function in <xref ref="pattern-exp-general-deriv" />.)
</p>

<identity xml:id="pattern-exp-integral">
	<title>Integral of an exponential function</title>

	<p>
	For rate function <m> r(t) = a^t </m>
	(with positive base <m> a </m>),
	we have <md> \ccmint{0}{t}{a^u}{u} = \frac{a^t - 1}{\ln (a)} </md>.
	A quantity experiencing an exponential rate of variation can itself be modelled by an exponential function
	<md> q(t) = q_0 + \frac{a^t - 1}{\ln (a)} </md>,
	where <m> q_0 = q(0) </m> is the initial amount.
	</p>

	<p>
	In the case of the natural exponential function as the rate function
	(that is, where <m> a = e </m>),
	then since <m> \ln(e) = 1 </m> we have <md> \ccmint{0}{t}{e^u}{u} = e^t - 1 </md>.
	</p>

</identity>

<figure xml:id="figure-exp-integral">
	<caption> Area under the natural exponential function is measured by the natural exponential function. </caption>
	<image width="62.0%">
		<shortdescription> Graph demonstrating that the area under the natural exponential function is measured by the natural exponential function. </shortdescription>
		<!-- TODO description -->
		<latex-image label="image-exp-natural-integral">
			<xi:include href="exp.d/natural-integral.tex" parse="text" />
		</latex-image>
	</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-exp-definite-integral">
	<title>Using <xref ref="pattern-exp-integral" /></title>
	<p>
	Let's extend <xref ref="example-log-definite-integral" />
	by computing
	<md> \ccmint{3}{4}{ t + \frac{1}{t} + e^t }{t} </md>.
	Use the same strategy as in <xref ref="example-log-definite-integral" />
	(and <xref ref="example-poly-definite-integral-mixed-exponents" />):
	<md>

		<mrow>
			\ccmint{3}{4}{ t + \frac{1}{t} + e^t }{t}
			\amp = \ccmint{3}{4}{t}{t} + \ccmint{3}{4}{\frac{1}{t}}{t} + \ccmint{3}{4}{ e^t }{t}
		</mrow>

		<mrow>
			\amp =
			\left(
				\ccmint{0}{4}{t}{t}
				- \ccmint{0}{3}{t}{t}
			\right)
		</mrow>
		<mrow>
			\amp \qquad {} +
			\left(
				\ccmint{1}{4}{\frac{1}{t}}{t}
				- \ccmint{1}{3}{\frac{1}{t}}{t}
			\right)
		</mrow>
		<mrow>
			\amp \qquad {} +
			\left(
				\ccmint{0}{4}{e^t}{t}
				- \ccmint{0}{3}{e^t}{t}
			\right)
		</mrow>

		<mrow>
			\amp =
			\left( \frac{4^2}{2} - \frac{3^2}{2} \right)
			+ ( \ln (4) - \ln (3) )
			+ \bbrac{ (e^4 - 1) - (e^3 - 1) }
		</mrow>

		<mrow> \amp = \frac{7}{2} + \ln \left(\frac{4}{3}\right) + e^3 (e - 1) </mrow>

	</md>.
	</p>
</example>

</section>

